  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/epalzeorhynchos-kalopterum-2/",
      "html": "<p>In the wild they reach 10-12 cm in length, while old aquarium specimens are said to have grown up to 16 cm. This is because fish grow throughout their lives (albeit slowly and little after reaching sexual maturity), and since such a Flying Fox will easily live up to 10-15 years in the aquarium (hardly ever over 3 years in the wild), they can grow quite large under certain circumstances.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/epalzeorhynchos-kalopterum-2/",
      "html": "<p>They are probably the most beautiful algae eaters of all. Offspring are offered from time to time, which is why wild catches are only relatively rarely imported. Adult wild specimens have recently been received via Singapore.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/epalzeorhynchos-kalopterum-2/",
      "html": "<p>The Flying Fox, Epalzeorhynchos kalopterum, originates from Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand, where it is a resident of flowing waters that carry deep dark brown black water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/epalzeorhynchos-kalopterum-2/",
      "html": "<p>Adult animals are rather to be classified as \"lazy\" algae eaters, in contrast to 3-4 cm long juveniles.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/08-carp-like-fishes-2-barbs-minnows-carps-goldfish-etc/epalzeorhynchos-kalopterum-2/",
      "html": "<p>Among themselves, the animals can be quarrelsome, which is why often only one specimen can be kept in smaller aquariums. In large tanks (from 150 cm), which are also furnished with a lot of root wood, this is much less unpleasant. Although the fish form a hierarchy, which is strictly observed, they are often found resting together in the same hiding place. If you want to try group keeping, there should be no fewer than 8 specimens, so that this behaviour can develop. Epalzeorhynchos kalopterum are usually peaceful towards fishes of other species as long as they do not look similar to the Flying Fox.</p>"
    }
  ],
